00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:59.380
caught in false signals or buying the top of a short-lived move. The strategy I call geopolitical

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:04.400
relief rally positioning is a short-to-medium term tactical approach designed to capture the sharp

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.520
equity rebounds that frequently follow credible signs of de-escalation in major international conflicts.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:15.420
We've seen this pattern repeat itself multiple times over the past decade, and today's pre-market

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:21.600
surge on Iran hopes is a textbook example of why the framework matters. Here's what typically happens,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:27.200
when a major conflict pushes energy prices and uncertainty higher, investors sell off risk assets

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:33.380
aggressively, creating an uncertainty premium in valuations. Once credible headlines suggest the conflict

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:38.800
may be ending, that premium shrinks rapidly and risk assets reprice in a compressed time frame,

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:44.740
often within the first five to ten trading days. The practical way to implement this is to identify

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:49.620
the sectors that sold off the hardest during the height of the fear. In the current environment,

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:55.120
those sectors include energy, defense, and certain industrials that are sensitive to both oil prices

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:01.920
and global supply chain stability. The tactic is to rotate into those beaten-down areas on confirmed

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.240
positive catalysts while simultaneously trimming positions in pure defensive sectors that had been

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:12.800
benefiting from the fear trade. Execution is straightforward on modern Canadian platforms like

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:18.840
Questrade or Interactive Brokers. I like to screen for names that showed the largest negative beta to

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:25.380
oil price spikes over the prior 30 days. On the relief day itself, I look for volume that is at least

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:33.340
1.5 times the 20-day average that confirms real conviction behind the move rather than low volume noise.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:40.800
Position sizing is critical here. I never put more than 3-5% of total portfolio equity into any

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:48.100
single relief rally name. I also set a relatively tight stop, typically 5-7% below the relief day low,

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.480
because these moves can reverse quickly if the hoped-for de-escalation falls apart.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:58.780
Historically, this approach delivered average gains of 4-12% in the sampled episodes following the

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:06.080
2022 and 2019-2020 flare-ups, but it does carry the obvious risk of false signals if fighting resumes.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:11.860
The key discipline is to treat the entire move as a tactical trade, not a fundamental rewrite of

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:17.000
the long-term outlook. Once the initial relief impulse starts to fade and volume normalizes,

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:23.040
it's time to take profits and move on. Mastering this requires a solid understanding of how liquidity

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.940
behaves in fast-moving geopolitical markets, which leads us directly into today's practical lesson

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:34.300
on trading costs during these volatile events. Let me paint a very realistic picture. Imagine you

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:40.380
decided to buy 500 shares of an energy ETF last week when oil prices spiked on the initial Iran

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:47.900
headlines. Your market order filled at $48.75, yet the ETF's official last traded price at that

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:54.560
exact moment showed only $48.40. What happened? The bid-ask spread had blown out dramatically,

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:59.880
from a normal 3 cents on quiet days all the way to $0.28 in the first 15 minutes of heavy

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:04.760
news flow. Your order essentially walked the book, paying up for liquidity that had temporarily

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:10.120
disappeared as market makers widened spreads to protect themselves. In today's environment,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:16.100
we saw the average energy sector ETF spread wide into 0.42 percent of price during the heaviest

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:23.640
war news hours compared with just 0.09 percent on normal trading days. That 0.33 percent slippage

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:31.180
on a $10,000 trade equals $33 evaporated instantly. On a round-trip trade, that hidden cost doubles.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:36.440
Professional traders always check the level 2 tape or the ETF's intraday indicative value

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:42.360
before hitting market orders during geopolitical spikes. The biggest mistake I see retail investors

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:48.020
make is treating all market orders as equal regardless of the volatility regime they're operating in.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:53.920
Instead, during these fast markets, I strongly recommend using limit orders placed within five to

00:06:53.920 --> 00:07:00.160
10 cents of the current national best bid or offer. On platforms that offer them, volume-weighted

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:07.220
average price, VWAP, or arrival price algorithms can also dramatically improve execution quality.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:12.580
Once you truly understand how spreads and temporary liquidity of operation work,

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:17.880
what used to be a hidden cost becomes a manageable variable you can actually plan around.

